From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:26:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] temporal/test: don't abort an update that was never started

TempoMapTest::addTest builds a private TempoMap with `new` (the
TempoMap::write_copy() call is commented out) but still finishes with
tmap->abort_update(). That calls SerializedRCUManager::abort(), which
unlocks the RCU writer std::mutex although no write_copy() locked it.
Unlocking a mutex the thread does not own is undefined behaviour: with
libstdc++/winpthreads it happens to be ignored, with libc++ on Windows
(SRW locks) it corrupts the lock, and the next test,
TempoMapTest::addRemoveTest, deadlocks in write_copy():

  #4 std::mutex::lock()
  #5 SerializedRCUManager<Temporal::TempoMap>::write_copy()
  #6 TempoMapTest::addRemoveTest()

Drop the unbalanced abort_update() call.
---
 libs/temporal/test/TempoMapTest.cc | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/temporal/test/TempoMapTest.cc b/libs/temporal/test/TempoMapTest.cc
index 98ff446..423d600 100644
--- a/libs/temporal/test/TempoMapTest.cc
+++ b/libs/temporal/test/TempoMapTest.cc
@@ -31,8 +31,10 @@ TempoMapTest::addTest()
 
 	CPPUNIT_ASSERT (tp.beats() == Beats (17,0));
 
-
-	tmap->abort_update ();
+	/* tmap is a private map, not obtained via TempoMap::write_copy(), so
+	 * there is no RCU write lock to release: calling abort_update() here
+	 * would unlock a std::mutex this thread does not hold.
+	 */
 }
 
 void
